  "contracts": {
    "pthm-td-unitary-entropy-invariance": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "pthm-nrqm-many-body-unitary-evolution-preserves-entropy",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "(a) *entropy is conserved*: for every declared sector preparation $\\rho(0)$\nof the model and every $t\\in\\mathbb R$,\n$$s(\\rho(t))=s(\\rho(0));$$\n\n(b) *the canonical preparation is stationary*:\n$U(t)\\rho_\\beta U(t)^*=\\rho_\\beta$ for every $t$, and every thermal mean\n$\\operatorname{tr}(\\rho_\\beta A)$ of an assigned observable is time\nindependent;\n\n(c) *no unitary production of the canonical preparation*: if\n$\\rho(0)\\ne\\rho_\\beta$ then $\\rho(t)\\ne\\rho_\\beta$ for every $t\\in\\mathbb R$;\nequivalently, no evolution of this isolated model maps a non-canonical\ndeclared preparation to the canonical one at any time;",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-nrqm-many-body-framework-entropy-and-free-energy",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "**(d) Physical entropy.** The **physical entropy** of $\\rho$ is\n$$S(\\rho):=k_B\\,s(\\rho),$$\nread in $\\mathrm J\\,\\mathrm K^{-1}$. It is nonnegative, is at most\n$k_B\\log D$, and vanishes exactly for pure preparations.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-td-macro-versus-micro-entropy-claim",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "These are distinct definitions on different domains: $S_c$ acts on an equilibrium macrostate, $S_{\\mathrm B}$ on a chosen macrocell, $S_{\\mathrm G}$ on a specified probability vector, and $S_{\\mathrm{vN}}$ on a finite-sector density operator. A numerical equality among them requires an additional, explicit identification of model, coarse-graining, preparation and applicable limit. This item supplies no such identification and adopts no canonical or microcanonical sampling rule.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-nrqm-many-body-framework-declared-fixed-n-model",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "energy reading and evolution.** In every realization with\n$\\mathfrak H_N^\\sigma\\ne\\{0\\}$, the pair $(H_N^\\sigma,\\mathrm J)$ is declared to be the **assigned energy\nobservable** of the $N$-particle sector in the sense of\n[[def-nrqm-observables-framework-observable-and-outcome-law]] clause (a): its\noutcome law in a declared sector preparation $\\rho$ is the law\n$\\mu^{H_N^\\sigma}_\\rho(B)=\\operatorname{tr}(\\rho E_{H_N^\\sigma}(B))$ of that\ndefinition clause (b), and its spectral data are those of the self-adjoint\noperator constructed in clause (d). The **model evolution** is the unitary\nflow $U(t)=\\exp(-itH_N^\\sigma/\\hbar)$ of\n[[def-nrqm-autonomous-dynamics-framework-evolution]] clause (b) with this\ngenerator. If $\\m",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-nrqm-framework-quantities-and-units",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "**(b) Reference units.** The **metre** (m), **second** (s), **kilogram** (kg) and **coulomb** (C) are the reference units of length, duration, mass and charge; a quantity of dimension $\\ell^{a}\\tau^{b}\\mu^{c}\\vartheta^{d}$ is expressed in the unit $\\mathrm m^{a}\\mathrm s^{b}\\mathrm kg^{c}\\mathrm C^{d}$. Coordinates of an inertial frame are read with the time coordinate $t$ in seconds, $[t]=\\tau$, and the spatial coordinates $x_{i}$ in metres, $[x_{i}]=\\ell$; slice distances of the arena are read in metres. In the temperature extension the reference unit of $\\Theta$ is the kelvin (K), so the general reference unit gains a factor $\\mathrm K^e$.\n\n**(c) Defining constants of the current SI.** The framework keeps $\\hbar$ explicit. In the current SI the constants $h$, $c$ and $e$ are exact defining constants, with the values $h=6{,}626\\,070\\,15\\times10^{-34}$ J s, $c=2{,}99792458\\times10^{8}$ m s$^{-1}$ and $e=1{,}602\\,176\\,634\\times10^{-19}$ C, and $\\hbar:=h/(2\\pi)$. These are real scalar constants with dimensions and units $[h]=[\\hbar]=\\mu\\ell^{2}\\tau^{-1}$ (J s), $[c]=\\ell\\tau^{-1}$ (m s$^{-1}$), and $[e]=\\vartheta$ (C). The vacuum permeability $\\mu_{0}$ is a positive real scalar that is experimentally determined in the current SI, with $[\\mu_{0}]=\\mu\\ell\\vartheta^{-2}$ (N A$^{-2}$ = H m$^{-1}$); $\\varepsilon_{0}:=(\\mu_{0}c^{2})^{-1}$ is a positive real scalar with $[\\varepsilon_{0}]=\\mu^{-1}\\ell^{-3}\\tau^{2}\\vartheta^{2}$ (F m$^{-1}$). These are unit-convention statements of the SI, not measurements reported by this framework; $c$ enters the framework only through the separate radiation assumptions adopted on their own pages, not in the nonrelativistic spacetime of [[def-nrqm-framework-newtonian-spacetime]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "def-nrqm-autonomous-dynamics-framework-evolution",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "a general preparation $\\rho$, that is an element of the state space $D(H)$ of [[def-nrqm-state-geometry-framework-state-space]], evolves by\n$$\\rho(t):=U(t)\\rho(0)U(t)^{*},$$\nthe reading of clause (iii) of P4 in the density-operator vocabulary of [[def-nrqm-state-geometry-pure-mixed-states-and-purity]] ([[def-self-adjoint-positive-unitary-and-normal-operator]], [[lem-nrqm-composites-density-and-scalar-spectral-probability]]). In this picture the assigned observables are held fixed at their assigned values and the preparation moves.\n\n**(d) Heisenberg picture.** For a **bounded** assigned sharp observable $(A,u_A)$ — that is, an assigned sharp observable with $A\\in\\mathcal B(H)$ ([[def-bounded-linear-operator]]),",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "def-nrqm-autonomous-dynamics-framework-evolution",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "**(b) The unitary flow.** The **time evolution** or **flow** of the isolated system is the family\n$$U(t):=\\exp\\!\\Bigl(-\\frac{i}{\\hbar}tH_{\\mathrm{op}}\\Bigr):=\\int_{\\mathbb R}e^{-it\\lambda/\\hbar}\\,dE_H(\\lambda)\\qquad(t\\in\\mathbb R),$$\nthe reading of clause (ii) of P4 in the spectral calculus of $H_{\\mathrm{op}}$ ([[def-unbounded-integral-against-a-pvm]]); here $\\hbar>0$ is the reduced Planck constant with its value and unit fixed by [[def-nrqm-framework-quantities-and-units]], so that $tH_{\\mathrm{op}}/\\hbar$ is dimensionless. Equivalently, $U(t)=e^{itT}$ for the self-adjoint generator $T:=-H_{\\mathrm{op}}/\\hbar$ with $D(T)=D(H_{\\mathrm{op}})$; the two readings agree by the uniqueness of the generator in Stone's",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "def-nrqm-many-body-framework-entropy-and-free-energy",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "**(f) Scope.** This definition covers only declared models with\nfinite-dimensional sector space, with the zero-sector branch of (a), and preparations in\n$\\mathcal D(\\mathfrak H_N^\\sigma)$. For a declared spatial realization, whose\nsector space is infinite-dimensional, the operator $\\rho\\log\\rho$ need not be\ntrace class; no entropy, physical entropy or free energy is defined here for\nthat case, and a separate trace-class statement would be required. The\ndefinition asserts no increase of entropy in time, no equilibration to\n$\\rho_\\beta$, no thermodynamic limit, no pressure or volume variable, and no\nmeasured temperature, entropy or free energy.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of [[def-choice-function]]: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets ([[def-countable]]) has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> ([[def-choice-function]]).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
