          "source": "rem-td-local-equilibrium-branch",
          "source_section": "Remark",
          "quote": "A **local-equilibrium approximation** is the model assumption that each coarse-grained cell at $(\\mathbf x,t)$ is represented by the equilibrium constitutive state labelled by $z(\\mathbf x,t)$. A mean free path $\\ell>0\\,\\mathrm m$ is the model's typical distance between scattering events; a field-variation length $L>0\\,\\mathrm m$ is the smallest declared spatial scale over which $z$ changes appreciably. A collision time $\\tau_{\\mathrm c}>0\\,\\mathrm s$ is a declared microscopic relaxation time, and a protocol time $\\tau_{\\mathrm m}>0\\,\\mathrm s$ is the timescale of imposed variation. A kinetic local-equilibrium regime may require $\\ell/L\\ll1$ and $\\tau_{\\mathrm c}/\\tau_{\\mathrm m}\\ll1$. These are qualitative reg",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "rem-td-local-equilibrium-branch",
          "source_section": "Remark",
